A major corporation is revising its employee handbook for entry-level staff. One proposal stresses that junior employees should follow directives from managers without debate, as unquestioning respect for hierarchy ensures efficiency and order. An alternative suggests allowing staff to respectfully raise concerns or seek clarification when instructions seem unclear or counterproductive.

            If you were advising company leadership, what would you recommend?:
            "A" = Endorse the second approach—thoughtful engagement improves outcomes more than rigid obedience.
"B" = Encourage respectful dialogue during team meetings, provided final decisions are accepted without resistance.
"C" = Permit clarification requests only in writing and after tasks are completed, to avoid disrupting workflow.
"D" = Adopt the first approach—new employees must learn that obeying authority is their primary responsibility.
"E" = Allow polite, private questions during onboarding but expect full compliance once training ends.

